 Convention on the Law of Treaties\nL6@P1: Done at Vienna on 23 May 1969\nL7@P1: The States Parties to the present Convention,\nL8@P1: Considering the fundamental role of treaties in the history of international relations,\nL9@P1: Recognizing the ever-increasing importance of treaties as a source of international law and as a\nL10@P1: means of developing peaceful cooperation among nations, whatever their constitutional and social\nL11@P1: systems,\nL12@P1: Noting that the principles of free consent and of good faith and the pacta sunt servanda rule are\nL13@P1: universally recognized,\nL14@P1: Affirming that disputes concerning treaties, like other international disputes, should be settled by\nL15@P1: peaceful means and in conformity with the principles of justice and international law,\nL16@P1: Recalling the determination of the peoples of the United Nations to establish conditions under\nL17@P1: which justice and respect for the obligations arising from treaties can be maintained,\nL18@P1: Having in mind the principles of international law embodied in the Charter of the United Nations,\nL19@P1: such as the principles of the equal rights and self-determination of peoples, of the sovereign equality and\nL20@P1: independence of all States, of non-interference in the domestic affairs of States, of the prohibition of the\nL21@P1: threat or use of force and of universal respect for, and observance of, human rights and fundamental\nL22@P1: freedoms for all,\nL23@P1: Believing that the codification and progressive development of the law of treaties achieved in the\nL24@P1: present Convention will promote the purposes of the United Nations set forth in the Charter, namely, the\nL25@P1: maintenance of international peace and security, the development of friendly relations and the\nL26@P1: achievement of cooperation among nations,\nL27@P1: Affirming that the rules of customary international law will continue to govern questions not\nL28@P1: regulated by the provisions of the present Convention,\nL29@P1: Have agreed as follows:\nL30@P1: PART I.\nL31@P1: INTRODUCTION\nL32@P1: Article 1\nL33@P1: Scope of the present Convention\nL34@P1: The present Convention applies to treaties between States.\nL35@P1-2: 2Article 2\nL36@P2: Use of terms\nL37@P2: 1. For the purposes of the present Convention:\nL38@P2: (a) “treaty” means an international agreement concluded between States in written form and governed\nL39@P2: by international law, whether embodied in a single instrument or in two or more related instruments and\nL40@P2: whatever its particular designation;\nL41@P2: (b) “ratification”, “acceptance”, “approval” and “accession” mean in each case the international act\nL42@P2: so named whereby a State establishes on the international plane its consent to be bound by a treaty;\nL43@P2: (c) “full powers” means a document emanating from the competent authority of a State designating a\nL44@P2: person or persons to represent the State for negotiating, adopting or authenticating the text of a treaty,\nL45@P2: for expressing the consent of the State to be bound by a treaty, or for accomplishing any other act with\nL46@P2: respect to a treaty;\nL47@P2: (d) “reservation” means a unilateral statement, however phrased or named, made by a State, when\nL48@P2: signing, ratifying, accepting, approving or acceding to a treaty, whereby it purports to exclude or to\nL49@P2: modify the legal effect of certain provisions of the treaty in their application to that State;\nL50@P2: (e) “negotiating State” means a State which took part in the drawing up and adoption of the text of\nL51@P2: the treaty;\nL52@P2: (f) “contracting State” means a State which has consented to be bound by the treaty, whether or not\nL53@P2: the treaty has entered into force;\nL54@P2: (g) “party” means a State which has consented to be bound by the treaty and for which the treaty is in\nL55@P2: force;\nL56@P2: (h) “third State” means a State not a party to the treaty;\nL57@P2: (i) “international organization” means an intergovernmental organization.\nL58@P2: 2. The provisions of paragraph 1 regarding the use of terms in the present Convention are without\nL59@P2: prejudice to the use of those terms or to the meanings which may be given to them in the internal law of\nL60@P2: any State.\nL61@P2: Article 3\nL62@P2: International agreements not within the scope\nL63@P2: of the present Convention\nL64@P2: The fact that the present Convention does not apply to international agreements concluded\nL65@P2: between States and other subjects of international law or between such other subjects of international\nL66@P2: law, or to international agreements not in written form, shall not affect:\nL67@P2-3: 3(a) the legal force of such agreements;\nL68@P3: (b) the application to them of any of the rules set forth in the present Convention to which they would\nL69@P3: be subject under international law independently of the Convention;\nL70@P3: (c) the application of the Convention to the relations of States as between themselves under\nL71@P3: international agreements to which other subjects of international law are also parties.\nL72@P3: Article 4\nL73@P3: Non-retroactivity of the present Convention\nL74@P3: Without prejudice to the application of any rules set forth in the present Convention to which\nL75@P3: treaties would be subject under international law independently of the Convention, the Convention\nL76@P3: applies only to treaties which are concluded by States after the entry into force of the present\nL77@P3: Convention with regard to such States.\nL78@P3: Article 5\nL79@P3: Treaties constituting international organizations and treaties\nL80@P3: adopted within an international organization\nL81@P3: The present Convention applies to any treaty which is the constituent instrument of an\nL82@P3: international organization and to any treaty adopted within an international organization without\nL83@P3: prejudice to any relevant rules of the organization.\nL84@P3: PART II.\nL85@P3: CONCLUSION AND ENTRY INTO FORCE OF TREATIES\nL86@P3: SECTION 1. CONCLUSION OF TREATIES\nL87@P3: Article 6\nL88@P3: Capacity of States to conclude treaties\nL89@P3: Every State possesses capacity to conclude treaties.\nL90@P3: Article 7\nL91@P3: Full powers\nL92@P3: 1. A person is considered as representing a State for the purpose of adopting or authenticating the\nL93@P3: text of a treaty or for the purpose of expressing the consent of the State to be bound by a treaty if:\nL94@P3: (a) he produces appropriate full powers; or\nL95@P3: (b) it appears from the practice of the States concerned or from other circumstances that their\nL96@P3: intention was to consider that person as representing the State for such purposes and to dispense with\nL97@P3: full powers.\nL98@P3-4: 42. In virtue of their functions and without having to produce full powers, the following are\nL99@P4: considered as representing their State:\nL100@P4: (a) Heads of State, Heads of Government and Ministers for Foreign Affairs, for the purpose of\nL101@P4: performing all acts relating to the conclusion of a treaty;\nL102@P4: (b) heads of diplomatic missions, for the purpose of adopting the text of a treaty between the\nL103@P4: accrediting State and the State to which they are accredited;\nL104@P4: (c) representatives accredited by States to an international conference or to an international\nL105@P4: organization or one of its organs, for the purpose of adopting the text of a treaty in that conference,\nL106@P4: organization or organ.\nL107@P4: Article 8\nL108@P4: Subsequent confirmation of an act performed\nL109@P4: without authorization\nL110@P4: An act relating to the conclusion of a treaty performed by a person who cannot be considered\nL111@P4: under article 7 as authorized to represent a State for that purpose is without legal effect unless afterwards\nL112@P4: confirmed by that State.\nL113@P4: Article 9\nL114@P4: Adoption of the text\nL115@P4: 1. The adoption of the text of a treaty takes place by the consent of all the States participating in\nL116@P4: its drawing up except as provided in paragraph 2.\nL117@P4: 2. The adoption of the text of a treaty at an international conference takes place by the vote of two\nL118@P4: thirds of the States present and voting, unless by the same majority they shall decide to apply a different\nL119@P4: rule.\nL120@P4: Article 10\nL121@P4: Authentication of the text\nL122@P4: The text of a treaty is established as authentic and definitive:\nL123@P4: (a) by such procedure as may be provided for in the text or agreed upon by the States participating in\nL124@P4: its drawing up; or\nL125@P4: (b) failing such procedure, by the signature, signature ad referendum or initialling by the\nL126@P4: representatives of those States of the text of the treaty or of the Final Act of a conference incorporating\nL127@P4: the text.\nL128@P4-5: 5Article 11\nL129@P5: Means of expressing consent to be bound by a treaty\nL130@P5: The consent of a State to be bound by a treaty may be expressed by signature, exchange of\nL131@P5: instruments constituting a treaty, ratification, acceptance, approval or accession, or by any other means\nL132@P5: if so agreed.\nL133@P5: Article 12\nL134@P5: Consent to be bound by a treaty expressed by signature\nL135@P5: 1. The consent of a State to be bound by a treaty is expressed by the signature of its representative\nL136@P5: when:\nL137@P5: (a) the treaty provides that signature shall have that effect;\nL138@P5: (b) it is otherwise established that the negotiating States were agreed that signature should have that\nL139@P5: effect; or\nL140@P5: (c) the intention of the State to give that effect to the signature appears from the full powers of its\nL141@P5: representative or was expressed during the negotiation.\nL142@P5: 2. For the purposes of paragraph 1:\nL143@P5: (a) the initialling of a text constitutes a signature of the treaty when it is established that the\nL144@P5: negotiating States so agreed;\nL145@P5: (b) the signature ad referendum of a treaty by a representative, if confirmed by his State, constitutes a\nL146@P5: full signature of the treaty.\nL147@P5: Article 13\nL148@P5: Consent to be bound by a treaty expressed by an\nL149@P5: exchange of instruments constituting a treaty\nL150@P5: The consent of States to be bound by a treaty constituted by instruments exchanged between them\nL151@P5: is expressed by that exchange when:\nL152@P5: (a) the instruments provide that their exchange shall have that effect; or\nL153@P5: (b) it is otherwise established that those States were agreed that the exchange of instruments should\nL154@P5: have that effect.\nL155@P5: Article 14\nL156@P5: Consent to be bound by a treaty expressed by ratification,\nL157@P5: acceptance or approval\nL158@P5: 1. The consent of a State to be bound by a treaty is expressed by ratification when:\nL159@P5-6: 6(a) the treaty provides for such consent to be expressed by means of ratification;\nL160@P6: (b) it is otherwise established that the negotiating States were agreed that ratification should be\nL161@P6: required;\nL162@P6: (c) the representative of the State has signed the treaty subject to ratification; or\nL163@P6: (d) the intention of the State to sign the treaty subject to ratification appears from the full powers of\nL164@P6: its representative or was expressed during the negotiation.\nL165@P6: 2. The consent of a State to be bound by a treaty is expressed by acceptance or approval under\nL166@P6: conditions similar to those which apply to ratification.\nL167@P6: Article 15\nL168@P6: Consent to be bound by a treaty expressed by accession\nL169@P6: The consent of a State to be bound by a treaty is expressed by accession when:\nL170@P6: (a) the treaty provides that such consent may be expressed by that State by means of accession;\nL171@P6: (b) it is otherwise established that the negotiating States were agreed that such consent may be\n"
